Subject: Privatization, goals, & visions

Phil Fraering: 
> As I've commented before to Nick, and I'm not sure he gets, the shape
> of an enterprise or "market" or whatever under a free system will bear
> _no_ relation whatsoever to its shape under socialism; it may do the
> same things much much better or other things altogether...

"_No_" is an exagerration, but I've been arguing the slightly
weaker point for years.  I take strong exception to talk of major 
improvements via privatization, where people assume private enterprise 
can bring magical improvements _without_ changing the shape.

One example Phil goes on to use: the example of a privately developed 
vs. government developed rocket. Too often, the privately developed 
rocket design, which strongly resembles publicly developed rockets in 
many of the mistaken ways (eg reusability via the most expensive high-tech 
like the Shuttle, emphasis on passengers despite lack of market 
demand for astronauts, etc.) magically comes in orders of magnitude 
lower cost than NASA's.  NASA is incompetent, but not by orders
of magnitude in tackling the same straightforward goals.
Private enterprise might greatly improve the industry, but not by 
magically throwing a wand across NASA and causing the same goals to be 
accomplished orders of magnitude cheaper.  Rather, the main advantage 
of private enterprise comes from choosing which goals can be best pursued 
today, and which can be best be put off until tommorrow.  This is where 
NASA's "visions" and "long-range planning" stray off the mark by many
orders of magnitude.  Its visions, and (via NASA contractor PR which
dominates the public perception) the visions of most space fans became 
obsoleted long ago, but without the market choosing winners and
tossing losers, there is no way to correct this gargantuan vision gap.



